<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mines and Works Act, 1911</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mines and Works Act of 1911 was a law of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> under which regulations reserved skilled jobs in the mines for white workers, the first of the industrial colour bar laws. When the courts struck down the regulations in 1923, Hertzog's Pact government restored the colour bar by an amending act of 1926. The act helped entrench a racial division of labour in South African mining.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Mines and Works Act, 1911, Act No. 12 of 1911, was enacted to set out the duties and responsibilities of workers in mines and works. It did not specify that Black South Africans should be discriminated against in any way; indeed it made no mention of race or colour, nor did it clearly give the government Mining Engineer powers to introduce a legal colour bar. Under section 4(n), however, it gave the Governor-General powers to grant, cancel and suspend certificates of competency for mine managers, overseers, surveyors, mechanical engineers, engine-drivers and miners entitled to blast, and to decide which other occupations required such certificates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The colour bar came through the regulations. A commission that had criticised mine managements for employing large numbers of unskilled whites nonetheless submitted, when it reported in 1910, draft regulations heavy with colour bars. The words white person were written into the definitions of banksman, onsetter, ganger and mine manager; only whites were to be employed in blasting, running elevators, driving engines and supervising boilers and other machinery, or as shift boss and mine overseer; and only whites could obtain the certificates of competence required of engine drivers and boiler attendants. With some changes these drafts became the Mines and Works Regulations, 1912.</p><p>The Mines and Works Amendment Act, 1926, Act No. 25 of 1926, placed Coloured workers beside whites in a position of privilege. Its key section required the minister, before announcing regulations for certificates of competency, including the blasting certificate, to seek through advisory committees the advice of the owners and of the organisations whose members held most of the certificates, that is, the white unions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The act, with or without its amendments, is sometimes called the Colour Bar or the Colour Bar Act, though the term colour bar usually refers to a wider group of labour practices, informal trade union practices, government regulations and laws, developed over time to keep Black workers from competing for jobs monopolised by whites. Further amending acts followed in 1931 and 1934. The Mines and Works Act, 1956 then repealed the earlier acts and their amendments; it still empowered the Governor-General to make regulations on certificates of competency for persons other than Black workers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mines and Works Act” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
